<p></p>
<p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></p>','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipinohomes-blog-xyzayape-4.png','views' => '74','category_id' => '16','published_at' => '2019-04-12 23:00:06','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '307','title' => '7 Catholic Churches in PAMPANGA for Visita Iglesia','slug' => '7-catholic-churches-in-pampanga-for-visita-iglesia','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Holy Week (or <em>Semana Santa or Mahal&nbsp;na&nbsp;Araw</em>) is upon us once again. One of the activities Catholics do during Maundy Thursday is <em>Visita Iglesia,</em> also known as The Seven Churches Visitation. &nbsp;We go on a church pilgrimage and visit 7 Christian sites or parishes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you\'re up for a road trip to Pampanga this Holy Week, here\'s a list of churches to visit:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<iframe src="https://www.google.com/maps/d/embed?mid=1z2myLsoHX9eOWOBGpxIdpuJZ3kLJECQB&amp;hl=en" width="800" height="600"></iframe>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1. Metropolitan Cathedral of San Fernando</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Sto. Rosario Poblacion, San Fernando City</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://scontent.fcgy1-1.fna.fbcdn.net/v/t1.0-9/21557446_1633203623397335_7910837939894626355_n.jpg?_nc_cat=105&amp;_nc_oc=AQk_wC7dvfdVH1CwYfHJiEuzNWi-tF9obPzmbLw9_5_P-q2_JMSH_pkhj7WRItYoj2U&amp;_nc_ht=scontent.fcgy1-1.fna&amp;oh=38a4e748463fb0a4bc882298afb61903&amp;oe=5D4BDEA2" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo credit: <a rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label=" (opens in a new tab)" href="https://www.facebook.com/SanFdoCathedral" target="_blank">https://www.facebook.com/SanFdoCathedral</a> <br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Metropolitan Cathedral of San Fernando, formerly Cathedral of Our Lady of the Assumption, is a neo-classical church in the provincial capital of Pampanga.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Metropolitan Cathedral was founded in 1754 by the Augustinians. The historical church was restored through the initiatives of Fernando Hizon Ocampo, a Kapampangan architect, after the stone church was burned by the Philippine Revolutionary Army on May 5, 1899 on orders of Gen. Antonio Luna. It was formally established as Metropolitan Cathedral on October 1, 1981 under the titularship of St. Ferdinand and Patronage of Our Lady of the Assumption. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2. San Guillermo Parish Church</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Cabambangan (Poblacion), Bacolor</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/San_Guillermo_Parish_Church_-_Bacolor%2C_Pampanga%2C_Philippines.jpg/1200px-San_Guillermo_Parish_Church_-_Bacolor%2C_Pampanga%2C_Philippines.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Jimvikipedia [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]<br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>San Guillermo Parish Church is named after San Guillermo, the patron saint of Bacolor.  It is now half-buried due to lahar in early 1990’s. The church was originally constructed by the Augustinian Friars in 1576.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>3. St. James the Apostle Parish Church (Betis Church)</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Betis, Guagua </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"linkDestination":"custom"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/projectkisame/10183274546/"><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/8533/10183274546_62d0cd8c3c_b.jpg" alt="Betis"/></a><figcaption>Photo Credit:  <a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/projectkisame/">Project Kisame</a><br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>St. James the Apostle Parish Church (commonly known as Betis Church) is a Baroque-style church located in the Betis District in Guagua. It was built in the 17th century, but it was in 1770 when it was finally rebuilt using concrete materials. Dubbed as the "Sistine Chapel of the Philippines," Betis Church was declared a National Cultural Treasure by the National Museum&nbsp;and the&nbsp;National Commission for Culture and the Arts. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>4. San Agustin Church (Lubao Church)</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9"> San Nicolas I, ‎Lubao </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Church_of_Lubao%2C_Lubao%2C_Pampanga.jpg/1200px-Church_of_Lubao%2C_Lubao%2C_Pampanga.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Joelaldor [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> The San Agustin parish church, also known as the Lubao Church, was declared as an <a href="https://www.asianjournal.com/life-style/arts-culture/national-museum-declares-441-year-old-church-in-lubao-as-important-cultural-property/">Important Cultural Property by the National Museum</a>.  The church was built in 1572 by Augustinian friars originally in Brgy. Sta. Catalina.  It was then transferred to San Nicolas I in 1602, due to continuous floods that swept Sta. Catalina.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:block {"ref":4063} /-->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Dalan Betis Rd, Santa Rita </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Sta_Rita_Church%2C_Pampanga.jpg/1200px-Sta_Rita_Church%2C_Pampanga.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Elmer B. Domingo [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sta. Rita de Cascia Parish Church is a heritage baroque church founded in 1726.  Fr. Francisco Royo built the present church in 1839; Fr. Juan Merino completed it in 1868. These two priests also opened the road linking Sta. Rita with Porac and Guagua.  “Polo” or forced labor, which was legal at that time, was used to expedite the construction.  <br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>6.  Santa Catalina de Alejandria Parish Church (Porac Church)  </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Blumanlan St, Porac</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/675Santa_Catalina_de_Alexandria_Parish_Church_20.jpg/1200px-675Santa_Catalina_de_Alexandria_Parish_Church_20.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Judgefloro [CC0]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Santa Catalina de Alejandria Parish Church, also known as the Porac Church, is a 19th-century Baroque church in Poblacion, Porac.  Its simple facade is adorned by Corinthian columns of its first and second levels, iron grills on the windows, the Augustinian emblem on the triangular pediment and rectangular pilasters. The main focal point of its exterior is the rose window bearing an image of Saint Catherine of Alexandria rendered on stained glass.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:block {"ref":4063} /-->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Santo Rosario St, Angeles</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Holy_Rosary_Parish_Church_%28Angeles_City%2C_Pampanga%29.jpg/1200px-Holy_Rosary_Parish_Church_%28Angeles_City%2C_Pampanga%29.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Ramon FVelasquez [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Holy Rosary Parish, formerly known as Santo Rosario Church and fondly called by <em>Kapampangans</em> as "Pisambang Maragul," which literally translates to "Big Church," stands in the heart of Angeles. From chapel made of bamboo, wood and nipa roof that founders Don Angel Pantaleon de Miranda and Doña Rosalia de Jesus built in what was then Barrio Culiat of San Fernando town, the church has evolved into a strong, crucifix-shaped Byzantine structure with two bell towers.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Holy Rosary Parish Church  Angeles, Philippines, was constructed from 1877 to 1896 by the "Polo y Servicio" labor system, a kind of forced labor imposed on Filipino peasants by the Spanish colonial government.  The church has been recognized by the National Commission for Culture and the Arts as a national historical site. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you need a little help with good ol\' Google maps, check this out:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<iframe src="https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=!1m64!1m12!1m3!1d493589.9261997738!2d120.5017011716998!3d14.871696880114916!2m3!1f0!2f0!3f0!3m2!1i1024!2i768!4f13.1!4m49!3e0!4m5!1s0x3397c8d26026386d%3A0x5fed23daac9278d0!2sMetro+Manila!3m2!1d14.609053699999999!2d121.0222565!4m5!1s0x3396f6db726d8419%3A0x883637a9dc86d6d8!2sMetropolitan+Cathedral+of+San+Fernando%2C+San+Fernando%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d15.0284968!2d120.6930225!4m5!1s0x3396f6237b19a149%3A0xbb0dd7b0cb7dcf49!2sSan+Guillermo+Parish+Church%2C+Bacolor%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d15.0005606!2d120.64853459999999!4m5!1s0x339658be2106561f%3A0xb0341a6cd2cfd005!2sBetis+Church%2C+Guagua%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d14.975589099999999!2d120.6427744!4m5!1s0x33965ec0dbbfd54b%3A0xa5a7885045fea29a!2sSaint+Augustine+Parish+Church%2C+Lubao%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d14.9386636!2d120.60177859999999!4m5!1s0x3396f5feff0d354f%3A0x5a2eec452d9ef699!2sSanta+Rita+de+Cascia+Church%2C+Dalan+Betis+Road%2C+Santa+Rita%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d14.999272099999999!2d120.61708209999999!4m5!1s0x3396f4b82f9dbf31%3A0x571f90e4ccffd92b!2sPorac+Church%2C+Blumanlan+Street%2C+Porac%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d15.072626!2d120.5396694!4m5!1s0x3396f24f232c8c0d%3A0x9806ef08494e895c!2sHoly+Rosary+Parish+Church%2C+Santo+Rosario+Street%2C+Angeles%2C+Pampanga!3m2!1d15.134216299999999!2d120.5902218!5e0!3m2!1sen!2sph!4v1554800579494!5m2!1sen!2sph" width="800" height="600" frameborder="0" style="border:0" allowfullscreen=""></iframe>
<!-- /wp:html -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipinohomes-blog-xyzayape-3.png','views' => '380','category_id' => '18','published_at' => '2019-04-15 13:30:45','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '308','title' => '7 Catholic Churches in DAVAO CITY for Visita Iglesia','slug' => '7-catholic-churches-in-davao-city-for-visita-iglesia','content' => '<p></p>
<p><!--StartFragment--></p>

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here in the Philippines, Catholics usually practice <em>Visita Iglesia,</em> visiting at least seven different churches on Maundy Thursday. If you are planning to be in Davao City for Holy Week, here are seven churches that you might want to include in your list:  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<iframe src="https://www.google.com/maps/d/embed?mid=1eq4EPFuCYpNSW2RosA7i1OKb-9ZeS7dX" width="800" height="600"></iframe>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1.  St. Mary of the Perpetual Rosary Church (Buhangin Shrine)&nbsp; </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Buhangin-Cabantian Road, Buhangin, Davao City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://2.bp.blogspot.com/-Pa-ZNl5ju9c/UC5QJDDPXaI/AAAAAAAABLo/hGZann8Uxbg/s1600/davao.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo Credit: <a href="http://traveljams.com">Travel Jams</a></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The St. Mary of Perpetual Rosary Church, also known as Buhangin Shrine, is famous for its string of steps leading up to the St. Mary’s Church. It\'s a perfect spot for meditation and reflection, away from the hustle and bustle of the city.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2.  Our Lady of Fatima Parish</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Fatima St., Cor. Aurora St., Davao City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://kulaiheartofagypsy.files.wordpress.com/2015/04/our-lady-of-fatima.jpeg?w=1024" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo Credit: <a href="https://kulaiheartofagypsy.wordpress.com/tag/davao-churches/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Heart of a Gypsy (opens in a new tab)">Heart of a Gypsy</a></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The first mass in the Our Lady of Fatima Parish was celebrated on February 11, 1953 by Fr. Lionelle Labelle, PME. It was then a wooden church that was completed in 1968. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>3.  Sta. Ana Shrine Parish</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Sta. Ana Avenue, Davao City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"linkDestination":"custom"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/projectkisame/10183274546/"><img src="https://www.justgola.com/media/a/00/02/10624_og_1.jpeg" alt="Betis"/></a><figcaption>Photo Credit:  <a href="https://www.justgola.com/a/sta-ana-shrine-2283286" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Danie Sedilla-Cruz (opens in a new tab)">Danie Sedilla-Cruz</a> <br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sta. Ana Parish Shrine, a semi-Gothic style church in Davao City, was consecrated  on April 16, 1949  by His Excellency Bishop Luis del Rosario (Zamboanga Diocese) even though it was not completely finished. The walls were of cement blocks. The windows (also Gothic style) were made of tiny pieces of glass, the colors of which mixed together harmoniously. The altar, in terazza, was a little jewel. The Last Supper Scene sculpted “in relief” constituted the main beauty of the altar. The statue of St. Anne was placed above the main altar.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Santa Ana Parish was detached from San Pedro Parish and canonically erected on July 26, 1949.   </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>4.  <strong>San Pedro Cathedral</strong>  </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Brgy. Poblacion District, Davao City  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/San_Pedro_Cathedral_on_a_Sunday_Afternoon.jpg/1200px-San_Pedro_Cathedral_on_a_Sunday_Afternoon.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Tubanismo [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]<br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The San Pedro Cathedral or Davao Cathedral (also referred to as St. Peter Metropolitan Cathedral), is a Roman Catholic cathedral dedicated to Saint Peter, and the ecclesiastical seat of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Davao.   This magnificent Spanish-style architecture with its distinctive modern design frontal-curved solid structure was first built using nipa and bamboo in 1847 and was subsequently rebuilt in wood in the mid 1900s by the late Architect Ramon Basa until it was finally remodelled in concrete in 1964 by Architect Manuel Chiew. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>5.  St. Jude Thaddeus Parish Church </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Gen. Malvar St, Poblacion District, Davao City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://jazperjay.files.wordpress.com/2014/12/dsc_0034.jpg?w=1050&amp;h=" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo Credit: <a href="https://jazperjay.wordpress.com/2014/12/12/st-jude-thaddeus-church/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="The Lone Traveller (opens in a new tab)">The Lone Traveller</a></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>St. Jude Thaddeus Parish Church was officially proclaimed as an independent parish in 1965 under the Archdiocese of Davao. The parish church architecture that we’re seeing nowadays was constructed in 1975.&nbsp; </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>6.    Shrine of the Holy Infant Jesus of Prague </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Matina, Davao City  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<a data-flickr-embed="true" href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/biagkensiak/8592172782" title="Shrine of the Holy Infant Jesus of Prague Davao City"><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/8241/8592172782_59664a0acf_b.jpg" width="1024" height="678" alt="Shrine of the Holy Infant Jesus of Prague Davao City"></a><script async="" src="//embedr.flickr.com/assets/client-code.js" charset="utf-8"></script>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Located atop Matina Hill, the Shrine of the Holy Infant Jesus of Prague is a local mecca for Catholic devotees, as well as being one of Davao City’s most famous landmarks and tourist spots. The structure is unusual in that its chapels are all open-air. During Holy Week, locals re-enact the Stations of the Cross on the hill leading up to the shrine.   <br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>7.  Our Lady of Lourdes Parish</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9"> Central Park Subdivision, Bangkal, Davao City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://2.bp.blogspot.com/-9InHIqm5S-I/U1EOiIQ_IPI/AAAAAAAAAUU/izB2okn22lQ/s1600/IMG_6115.JPG" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo Credit: <a href="http://noelasarza.blogspot.com/2014/04/churches-we-visited-for-bisita-iglesia.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Dock &amp; Ride (opens in a new tab)">Dock &amp; Ride</a></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The new church that now stands on the main road of Central Park, the permanent home of the Our Lady of Lourdes Parish, is a visible example of the wonderful spirit that has united the community through the loving and caring work of all parishioners together with all the priest who served the parish. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<p><!--EndFragment--></p>
<p></p>
<p><!--EndFragment--></p>
<p></p>','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipinohomes-blog-xyzayape-4-1.png','views' => '38','category_id' => '18','published_at' => '2019-04-16 03:00:07','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '309','title' => '7 Catholic Churches Around BACOLOD for Visita Iglesia','slug' => '7-catholic-churches-around-bacolod-for-visita-iglesia','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em>Visita Iglesia</em>, the practice of visiting at least seven different churches on Maundy Thursday, is one of the most popular Holy Week sacred vows (or <em>panata</em>) that Filipino Catholics do.&nbsp; And often, we take this time to enjoy a bit of a road trip with the family. If you are planning to do a Visita Iglesia around Bacolod, here are seven churches that you might want to include in your list:  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:html -->
<iframe src="https://www.google.com/maps/d/embed?mid=1xe6E8Bg7pOyQ-dnDGaNLcn1b_0-UO1DK" width="800" height="600"></iframe>
<!-- /wp:html -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1. Chapel of Cartwheels</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Manapla</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Chapel_of_Cartwheels.JPG/1200px-Chapel_of_Cartwheels.JPG" alt=""/><figcaption>Elmer B. Domingo [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Chapel_of_Cartwheels_Interior.JPG/1200px-Chapel_of_Cartwheels_Interior.JPG" alt=""/><figcaption>Elmer B. Domingo [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Chapel of Cartwheels in Hacienda Rosalia was built in the 1960s. This small, unique place of worship is made from farm implements such as cartwheels, plows, mortar and pestle, margaha sand, and broken pieces of glass. It was patterned like a "salakot" or a traditional wide-brimmed hat. The altar is a huge centuries-old boulder, and the religious images are carved out of wood. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2.  St. Joseph the Worker Parish </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Victorias City</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/St._Joseph_the_Worker_Church_facade%2C_Victoria_City%2C_Negros_Occidental.jpg/1280px-St._Joseph_the_Worker_Church_facade%2C_Victoria_City%2C_Negros_Occidental.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Aerous">Elmer B. Domingo</a>&nbsp;-&nbsp;Own work  [<a rel="noreferrer noopener" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0" target="_blank">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>]<br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/The_Angry_Christ_Mural_of_the_Saint_Joseph_The_Worker_Parish_Church.jpg/1200px-The_Angry_Christ_Mural_of_the_Saint_Joseph_The_Worker_Parish_Church.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>MonteSacro8 [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The St. Joseph the Worker Chapel (commonly known as the Angry Christ Church) is located inside the Victorias Milling Company residential complex in Victorias City.  It was built between 1948-50 by the Ossorio family. The church is considered as the first example of modern sacral architecture in the Philippines, and was declared Important Cultural Property of the Philippines in 2015.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>3.  San Diego Pro-Cathedral </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Silay City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"linkDestination":"custom"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/projectkisame/10183274546/"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/San_Diego_Pro-Cathedral.JPG/1200px-San_Diego_Pro-Cathedral.JPG" alt="Betis"/></a><figcaption>Elmer B. Domingo [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]<br></figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/San_Diego_Pro-Cathedral_02.JPG/599px-San_Diego_Pro-Cathedral_02.JPG" alt=""/><figcaption>ClaireMRA [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The San Diego Pro-cathedral is an early 20th-century church in Silay City. It was formerly known as the San Diego Parish Church or the St. Didacus Parish Church prior to its declaration as a pro-cathedral in 1994. It was established in 1776, with its first church built of light materials. In 1925, work began on a grander structure to replace the old church. It was completed in 1927.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>4.  San Nicolas de Tolentino Parish  </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Talisay City  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a1/Talisay_City_Church%2C_Negros_Occidental.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Joelaldor [CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>San Nicolás de Tolentino Parish was founded on 1 January 1850, when the Augustinian Recollect Father Fernando Cuenca, as the first parish priest, took possession of the vast “cogon wasteland” named Minuluan.     </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>5. San Sebastian Cathedral </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9">Bacolod City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/San_Sebastian_Cathedral_Bacolod.jpg/1200px-San_Sebastian_Cathedral_Bacolod.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Hbalairos [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/IJVBacolodCathedral2ver2.jpg/1200px-IJVBacolodCathedral2ver2.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>IJVelas [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The San Sebastian Cathedral is a late 19th-century baroque-style church in Bacolod City. The present structure was completed and inaugurated on January 20, 1882. It was consecrated in 1933.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>6.   Our Lady of Guadalupe Church</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Valladolid </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Our_Lady_of_Guadalupe_Church_Valladolid_Negros_Occ_Philippines.jpg/1200px-Our_Lady_of_Guadalupe_Church_Valladolid_Negros_Occ_Philippines.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Paolobon140 [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Our Lady of Guadalupe Church is one of the oldest churches in Negros Island. It was built 1851-1852 by the Recollect Missionaries. It is made of coral stones.   <br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>7.  Our Lady of Peace Parish  </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4 id="mce_9"> La Carlota City  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/La_Carlota_Church_External_view.jpg/1200px-La_Carlota_Church_External_view.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Hbalairos [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/La_Carlota_Church.jpg/1200px-La_Carlota_Church.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Hbalairos [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)]</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Our Lady of Peace Parish was built in 1870 using light materials (mainly bamboo and heath). The present structure, which is mainly made of coral stones and bricks, was started in 1876 and completed in 1877. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<p><!--EndFragment--></p>
<p></p>','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipinohomes-blog-xyzayape-5.png','views' => '37','category_id' => '18','published_at' => '2019-04-16 23:00:52','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '310','title' => 'Are Real Estate Developers Allowed to Collect Fees for Community Benefit?','slug' => 'are-real-estate-developers-allowed-to-collect-fees-for-community-benefit','content' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>The answer is NO.</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>But why are we paying for additional fees and maintenance in condominiums and subdivisions? </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4098,"width":588,"height":345} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image is-resized"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/download-4.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4098" width="588" height="345"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you count to check your receipts or invoice the monthly dues that you are paying for are from Homeowners Association Fees. Homeowners Association is a community built organization, created and governed by homeowners or co-owners of the subdivision you are in. Prior to purchasing the property, you are informed of the existence of this association to help maintain peace and camaraderie among homeowners. [<a href="http://localhost:3001/condominium-homeowners-dues/">To learn more about Homeowners Association and Condo Dues click here</a>] </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Any collections for said purpose may only be done by the duly organized homeowners association but with the consent of the actual residents in the project.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>What are these fees for?</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Homeowners association dues that are usually paid monthly or annually covers the following;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":3912} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/2615008_17110707250058801684-1024x576.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-3912"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>Subdivision/Condominium maintenance of the common areas.</li><li>Community Security Guards and CCTV\'s</li><li>Garbage Disposal and Facilities</li><li>Community Club House and Amenities</li><li>Community Gardens and the likes</li><li>Other fees that are necessary for community living</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Section 27 of PD 957 provides, thus:</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>“SECTION 27. Other Charges. – No owner or developer shall levy upon any lot or unit buyer a fee for an alleged community benefit. Fees to finance services for common comfort, security and sanitation may be collected only by a properly organized homeowners association and only with the consent of a majority of the lot or unit buyers actually residing in the subdivision or condominium project.”</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Have you been paying for subdivision or condominium dues? How orderly is your community? </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipinohomes-blog-xyzayape-2-1.png','views' => '67','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-04-18 03:00:33','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '311','title' => 'Highly Recommended Investment Opportunities in Bacolod','slug' => 'highly-recommended-investment-opportunities-in-bacolod','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Bacolod is a highly urbanized city on the northwest coast of Negros Island in the Philippines. It is part of the Bacolod Metropolitan Area, which also includes the cities of Silay and Talisay. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://filipinohomes.com/tourism/bacolod/assets/images/bacolod-c.jpg" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>With the investment uptrend in Negros Occidental in recent years, which is expected to continue in 2019 and even beyond, it\'s no surprise that Bacolod is becoming an attractive city to invest in. Some investments from the property giants Ayala Land, Inc. and Megaworld Corp., along with Bacolod Real Estate Development Corp.’s opening of its new passenger terminal, SM Prime Holdings Inc.’s hotel development, and the start of economic highway construction in the province [<a href="http://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1057929 ">src]</a>, make the City of Smiles a fertile ground for more investors.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Read more:&nbsp; </strong><a rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label=" (opens in a new tab)" href="https://filipinohomes.com/bacolod" target="_blank">BACOLOD by Filipino Homes</a> </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you are looking for income opportunities in rental real estate, here are some highly recommended properties:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>MesaVirre Garden Residences</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>by Cebu Landmasters</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://www.cebulandmasters.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/10/MesaVirre-Render-with-LTS-768x566.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>HLURB NO.: LS-WVR-18-0335501 (PHASE 1), LS-WVR-18-0335515 (PHASE 2) </figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>MesaVirre Garden Residences</strong> is the 5th of Cebu Landmasters, Inc\'s garden residences series, following its success in Cebu, Cagayan de Oro and Davao City.  It is a mixed-use condominium on a one-hectare property strategically located along Lacson Street, a lifestyle hub in Bacolod. It is just a few minutes away from Robinsons Place and Central District IT Park. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>Amaia Steps Capitol Central</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>by Amaia Land Corp, wholly-owned subsidiary of&nbsp;Ayala Land Inc.  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://www.amaialand.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Capitol-Central-9.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption> <br>HLURB NO.: WVR – AA- 2017/05-011 </figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Amaia Steps Capitol Central </strong>is the first mid-rise residential development of Amaia Land Corp in Bacolod. Located at San Juan Street., Brgy. 8, the property enjoys the privilege of having its own support retail shops for the convenience of its residents. Amenities include swimming pools for adults and kids, kids’ play area, landscaped garden, and a multi-purpose hall. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Featuring its North Building with 9-storey development, Amaia Steps Capitol Central will have 288 units of well designed Studio, De Luxe, and Premier units.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>One Regis</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>by Megaworld Corporation</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://www.megaworldinternational.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/One-Regis-1024x576.png" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>One Regis is a mid-rise residential condominium, which offers units ranging from studios (up to 37 square meters), one-bedroom (up to 58 square meters), and two-bedrooms (up to 73 square meters). Some units feature an exclusive balcony overlooking either the township’s landscape or the amenity deck’s pool and indoor park.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The second floor’s amenity deck will have facilities such a swimming pool and a kiddie pool, pool lounge and seating areas, fitness center, events hall, male and female shower rooms, and a walk park.

</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Two Regis</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>by Megaworld Corporation</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://megaworld-pe8k1ip.stackpathdns.com/sites/default/files/2019-02/two-regis-the-upper-east-bacolod-image.jpg" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Six months after the successful launch of One Regis, Megaworld announced another condominium in its Upper East township. Two Regis is a 14-storey residential tower that will be built at the corner of Regis and Madison Streets within the 34-hectare township in the eastern part of Bacolod City.  Strategically located just beside the proposed Megaworld Lifestyle Mall, Two Regis will offer units ranging from Studio (up to 31.5 square meters); Executive Studio (up to 37.5 square meters); One-Bedroom (up to 43.5 square meters) and Two-Bedroom (up to 87 square meters). Several available units will have their own balcony, overlooking the transforming landscapes of the township. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Expected to be completed by 2023.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape.png','views' => '64','category_id' => '15','published_at' => '2019-04-29 07:00:31','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '312','title' => 'PAG-IBIG | Why is my property tagged as foreclosed?','slug' => 'pag-ibig-why-is-my-property-tagged-as-foreclosed','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>PAG-IBIG Fund is a government-mandated Home Development Mutual Fund, that gives its contributing members the power to&nbsp;<em>finance</em> their home purchase at a more affordable rate, as compared to the rates offered by real estate developers and banks. This gives every Filipino an opportunity to own a home without having to pay in bulk amount and with lower interest rates.  However, there are borrowers who fail to pay for their loaned property and gets their properties foreclosed. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Pag-ibig Foreclosed properties mean that Pag-ibig Fund takes possession of a mortgaged property as a result of the mortgagor \'s failure to keep up their mortgage payments. These are properties acquired by Pag-ibig due to a non-paying borrower. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When you take up loan through Pag-ibig Fund, they take your property as a collateral of the borrowed money used to pay for the property to your property developer. In the event, you don\'t make payments of your monthly amortization, PAG-IBIG Fund or the bank where you borrowed money from will take over your property. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>How many months before they declare my property foreclosed?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Non-payment of your monthly amortization for <strong>3 months</strong>, your account will already be tagged as foreclosed. Your account will be forwarded to a 3rd party collecting agency that will contact you to remind you about your unpaid account. After a few consecutive communication, if as a borrower you will not commit to pay or negotiate to pay for the property they will endorse your account to a sheriff for you to vacate the property. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Does the bank or PAG-IBIG or financing institution have the right to take over the foreclosed property? </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Yes, it is stated in the contract upon the loan approval and release of your loan. You signed and agreed to it, prior to moving in. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In all foreclosed properties, the first priority to buy back the property is the original owner of the property. If after posting of the foreclosed properties and the first owner will not show any interest in buying it back, the financing institution has the right to award the property to the next eligible buyer. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you are interested to purchase a foreclosed property in Pag-ibig, Visit <a href="https://www.pagibigfund.gov.ph/aa/search.aspx"><strong>PAG-IBIG ACQUIRED ASSETS</strong></a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Although it is true that no one is put in jail for unpaid debts, but you will surely loose a property if you continue not to pay a loan. Whatever your personal reason is, a debt is a debt and you have to pay it, whether you like it or not. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape-1.png','views' => '212','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-04-30 14:33:29','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '313','title' => 'What is Pagibig Overseas Program (POP)','slug' => 'what-is-pagibig-overseas-program-pop','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>People really get confused about this PAG-IBIG membership program; the PAG-IBIG OVERSEAS PROGRAM. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Pag-Ibig Overseas Program is a Provident (Savings) Program of Pag-Ibig Fund for our OFWs.  Pag-IBIG Fund guarantees the refund of member\'s total accumulated savings (TAV), which consists of the member\'s accumulated contributions, the employer counterpart contributions if any, and the dividend earnings credited to the member\'s account upon the occurrence of any of the following grounds for withdrawal: <br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>1. Membership maturity. </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The member must have remitted at least 240 monthly membership contributions with the Fund. For Pag-IBIG Overseas Program (POP) members, membership with the Fund shall be at the end of five (5), ten (10), fifteen (15), or twenty (20) years depending on the option of the member upon membership registration.<br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>2.Retirement. </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The member shall be compulsorily retired upon reaching age 65. He may <g class="gr_ gr_8 gr-alert gr_gramm gr_inline_cards gr_run_anim Punctuation only-ins replaceWithoutSep" id="8" data-gr-id="8">however</g>, opt to retire upon the occurrence of any of the following<g class="gr_ gr_9 gr-alert gr_gramm gr_inline_cards gr_run_anim Style replaceWithoutSep" id="9" data-gr-id="9">:Actual</g> retirement from the SSS, the GSIS or a separate employer provident/retirement plan, provided the member has at least reached age 45.Upon reaching age 60.<br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>3.Permanent and Total Disability or Insanity. </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The following disabilities shall be deemed total and permanent:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>Temporary total disability lasting continuously for more than 120 days;</li><li>Complete loss of sight of both eyes;</li><li>Loss of two limbs at or over the ankle or wrist;</li><li>Permanent complete paralysis of two limbs;</li><li>Brain injury resulting in incurable imbecility or insanity; and </li><li>Such other cases which are adjudged to be total and permanent disability by a duly licensed physician and approved by the Board of Trustees.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>4. Separation from the service due to health reason</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>5. Permanent departure from the country</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>6. Death. </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In case of death, the Fund benefits shall be divided among the member\'s legal heirs in accordance with the New Civil Code as amended by the New Family Code.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:table {"hasFixedLayout":true,"className":"is-style-regular"} -->
<table class="wp-block-table has-fixed-layout is-style-regular"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Effects of <a href="https://senate.gov.ph/republic_acts/ra%209679.pdf">RA 9679 </a>on OFW Pag-IBIG members</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>How does my POP membership differ from that under RA 9679?<br></strong><br>The Pag-IBIG Overseas Program is entirely different from Pag-IBIG I. POP was a voluntary savings scheme for Filipinos abroad while membership under Pag-IBIG I is mandatory for Filipinos working abroad by virtue of RA 9679.<br><br><strong>Do I have the option not to register under Pag-IBIG I since I am already a POP member?<br></strong><br>Under RA 9679, all Overseas Filipino Workers are required to register under Pag-IBIG I, regardless if they are POP members or not. Other POP members (immigrants and those naturalized in other countries) may register voluntarily under Pag-IBIG I.<br><br><strong>I am an OFW and a POP member as well, do I have the option to stop my POP membership and re-register for Pag-IBIG I membership instead?</strong><br><br>Yes, you may discontinue your POP membership, provided you do not have an existing housing loan with Pag-IBIG Fund.<br><br><strong>Can I continue with my POP membership and be a member under RA 9679 simultaneously?</strong><br><br>Yes, a POP member is required to register under Pag-IBIG I but, at the same time, he may also opt to continue his POP membership.<br><br><strong>When can I withdraw my POP TAV if I stop my POP contributions and register under RA 9679?</strong><br><br>POP TAV may be withdrawn at point of maturity of POP membership.<br><br><strong>Will my POP TAV and TAV under RA9679 be merged?</strong><br><br>No, your TAV under both programs shall be kept separate. Your POP TAV will be released to you upon maturity of term even if your Pag-IBIG I membership is not yet due for release.<br></td></tr></tbody></table>
<!-- /wp:table -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Pagibig OFW Remittance Centers Abroad https://www.pagibigfund.gov.ph/pop/</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => 'What is Pag-Ibig Overseas Program? ','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filiipinohomes-blog-xyza-yape-real-estate-ph.png','views' => '66','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-05-02 05:00:26','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '314','title' => 'Things you should watch out for when dealing with developers','slug' => 'things-you-should-watch-out-for-when-dealing-with-developers','content' => 'Purchasing a property from a developer can be an exciting at the same time a nerve-wracking experience. Of course, you always wanted to have the best offer and makes sure to get the right ones. However, you need to be cautious enough because there are developers that can steer you into a much longer and broader game that’s considerably less advantageous for you. These profit-minded developers usually target those with less knowledge about the industry.

Here’s what you need to watch out for.
<ul>
 	<li>
<h3>Legality Matters</h3>
</li>
</ul>When dealing one, it is of vital importance to do an honest-to-goodness check if the project of your choice is registered and licensed by the Housing and Land Use Regulatory Board (HLURB). To do such, you may use HLURB Online Services<a href="/#_ftn1" name="_ftnref1">[1]</a>or ask them directly. If, not then it just shows that the developer has failed to comply numerous requirements of HLURB. Profit-wise, in business it is always been a motto and there is nothing wrong with that as long as it is done legally, just make sure that the property you will be investing serves it purpose and not just the developer selling it to you.
<ul>
 	<li>
<h3>Reputed Track record</h3>
</li>
</ul>It is a common misconception to any that buying a property from a well-known developer is “safe” however it doesn’t mean that they are known because, in reality, they hire different subcontractors and all of which are of varying quality. There is also the issue on delayed turnover of land titles especially on investment properties thus you may verify if the developer has a track record of at least 3 major projects that have been turned over to the owners on time.
<ul>
 	<li>
<h3>Quality Standards</h3>
</li>
</ul>Some of today’s developers take a shortcut which means when you buy a property especially for investment, they will just do it in a short time period for what? Quick profits. For example, a case was filed by new tenants of a subdivision in Bulacan regarding unlawful site digging, causing the foundation of their houses to lose its strength and stiffness in response to the applied stress. To do this, you may personally visit the subdivision/condominium, where the house and lot or condo unit to be purchased is located. Also check who would pay the cost of the water, electric meters, the subdivision perimeter fence, and so on.
<ul>
 	<li>
<h3>Breach of Contract</h3>
</li>
</ul>For most common example, if it includes automatic membership of the Homeowner’s Association even though your purchased property is still under development; watch out, developers might trick you. It is always illegal under the law for a developer to take hold of the association and it’s a known fact that an amenity is supposed to be free as it is already part of the premium purchase. It is also advisable to read Presidential Decree 957, or the Subdivision and Condominium Buyer’s Protective Decree (as amended by PD 1216), the decree that involves regulating the sale of subdivision lots and condominiums, and provides penalties for violations.
<ul>
 	<li>
<h3>Unexplained restrictions</h3>
</li>
</ul>You should always verify the ‘contract to sell’ details and fully understand the terms and conditions as part of the developers’ guaranteed amenities to your property purchase. Make sure that they don’t have any policy of prohibiting you to inspect the property while under construction and make sure everything seems forthcoming.

<a href="/#_ftnref1" name="_ftn1">[1]</a>http://hlurb.gov.ph/online-inquiry/','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2018/06/img_9150-1.jpg','views' => '57','category_id' => '13','published_at' => '2019-05-16 07:33:59','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '315','title' => 'What you should do before buying a resell property?','slug' => 'what-you-should-do-before-buying-a-resell-property','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Are you planning to buy a property and wondering if it would be better to buy a new home or an old house? There are a lot of things to consider when choosing a new home, one of that is either to buy a newly constructed home from developer or buy an old property from a family who may have used it years to decades before that.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>What to check if you\'re buying an old or resell home or property</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Authentic “Transfer Certificate of Title”</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":494} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/Spotting-a-Fake-TitleNew-1024x806.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-494"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Getting a “Certified True Copy” of the title from the Register of Deeds is the easiest way to check if the title to the property you are buying is authentic. Ask the seller of the property for a photocopy of the title so that you will the necessary information (title number and the name of the owner) to get a certified true copy of the title from the Register of Deeds.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Clean Title</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://philnews.ph/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/land-title.jpg" alt=""/><figcaption>Photo from: Philnews.ph</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Make sure that the property is not mortgaged  and has no debts. Check at the back of the title with the heading “Encumbrances” and make sure that it is empty if you are told that the title is “clean”. You might find technical description of the property from the front page of the title and the description of the property is continued on the “Encumbrances” page, this is of course all right.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Correct Property on the Title</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Land titles don’t have any street name and number to pin point a property, it is a must to validate actual property you are buying matches the technical description on the Transfer Certificate of Title by hiring a Property Surveyor.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Real Owners</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Ask for identification papers like passport or driver’s license to make sure you are talking to the real owner.If you can, talk to the neighbors to confirm the identity of the sellers, as well as some history of the property.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Updated Payment of <a href="http://localhost:3001/real-estate-taxes-why-do-i-need-to-pay-for-it/">Real Estate Taxes</a></strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Ask for a copy of the Tax Declaration and Tax Receipts to confirm that real estate tax payments are up to date.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/05/Copy-of-filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape.png','views' => '60','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-05-19 22:00:34','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '316','title' => 'Why You Should Care About The Properties of Your Deceased Parents?','slug' => 'why-you-should-care-about-the-properties-of-your-deceased-parents','content' => '<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://heavensmaid.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/people-put-flowers-graves_17413cefbbf774cb-750x422.jpg" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The death of a family member is a difficult time for anyone; most specially if it is our parents. One of the last thing you want to think about are the practical and legal steps you need to prepare.  One of the legal process that you may work on in the event of a deceased is settling an estate, or estate settlement. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p><strong>READ:</strong> <a href="http://localhost:3001/will-testament-why-is-it-important/"><em>Will &amp; Testament | Why is it important?</em></a></p></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2><strong>Manage and Settle the Estate</strong></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>An “estate” in legal terms, is the collection of assets left behind by a decedent. The <a href="http://localhost:3001/extrajudicial-settlement-estate-work/">estate settlement</a> process is the legal process of disposing or transferring of the assets and addressing any other questions or legal issues that might arise.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Real property can not be transferred from the deceased to his or her heirs without the<a href="http://localhost:3001/transfer-title-deceased-parents/"> filing of the Estate Tax Return and payment of the Estate Tax</a>. <br><br>It is not an unusual story to hear about heirs realizing the need to pay the Estate Tax after years or even decades have their parents have passed away.  For example, a parent died in 2009 and if the estate tax is paid on time would have been around P40,000 but the heirs did not see it important to settle it on time. If the heirs is approached by potential buyer of the property today, they will have to settle the Estate Tax before the property can be sold. The P40,000 Estate Tax at the time of death will now inflate and with additional penalties it will be three to four times more than the initial amount.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p><strong>READ:</strong><a href="http://localhost:3001/transfer-title-deceased-parents/"><em>Transferring a Property Title From Deceased Parents in PH</em></a></p></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4144} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/estate-tax-1024x683.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4144"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Non-payment of Estate Tax is common in the Philippines. This brings problems when the properties need to be transferred to the names of buyers. To avoid this situation, it is advisable for the heirs to settle the Estate Tax before the deadline. If they do not have enough cash, an extension of time to pay can be requested or apply for installment payment.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This year is the best time to start planning for that if you have estates that are not yet settled. Here\'s a recent article about the Estate Tax Amnesty recently signed by the President <strong><a href="https://www.philstar.com/business/2019/04/22/1911392/bir-finalize-estate-tax-amnesty-rules-may">CLICK HERE.</a></strong> </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/05/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape.png','views' => '39','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-05-20 22:00:01','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '317','title' => 'Feature Story | Eduardo G. Ong, PhD, DPA, DBA: Father of Philippine Real Estate','slug' => 'father-of-philippine-real-estate','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>  <br>Dr. Eduardo G. Ong, the “<em>Father of Philippine Real Estate,</em>”  is currently the chairman of the Professional Regulatory Board of Real Estate Service (PRB-RES), which spearheads the implementation of the Real Estate Service Act (RESA).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":3965} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/31823196_2000909090173216_964587880166981632_n.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-3965"/><figcaption>Photo Source:   <a href="https://www.facebook.com/dregong111/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label=" (opens in a new tab)">Dr. Eduardo G. Ong, PhD, DPA, DBA</a> </figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>He is a highly respected International Management Consultant, Realtor, Educator, and Business Coach.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Dr. Ong has six (6) degrees to his credits: BS Commerce (1967, University of Santo Tomas); Master of Science in Commerce (1975 , University of Santo Tomas); a Law Degree (1995, Philippine Law School) and Three (3) Doctorate Degrees – Doctor of Philosophy Major in Commerce (1985, University of Santo Tomas), Doctor of Public Administration (2005, Trinity University of Asia); and Doctor in Business Administration (2008, <br>Eulogio “Amang” Rodriguez Institute of Science and Technology (EARIST)). He was also awarded an Honorary PhD degree from the Universidad PanAmericana during its Asian Graduation Ceremony held at the Macau Tower, Macau, China (2018) and Doctorate Fellow from the Academy of Multi-Skills (2019). </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:gallery {"ids":[4171,4172,4173]} -->
<ul class="wp-block-gallery columns-3 is-cropped"><li class="blocks-gallery-item"><figure><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/44574734_1451606651649484_6592134844937928704_n.jpg" alt="" data-id="4171" data-link="https://filipinohomes.com/blog/?attachment_id=4171" class="wp-image-4171"/></figure></li><li class="blocks-gallery-item"><figure><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/22688322_1969733449971257_7707418397376351945_n.jpg" alt="" data-id="4172" data-link="https://filipinohomes.com/blog/?attachment_id=4172" class="wp-image-4172"/></figure></li><li class="blocks-gallery-item"><figure><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/edong.jpg" alt="" data-id="4173" data-link="https://filipinohomes.com/blog/?attachment_id=4173" class="wp-image-4173"/></figure></li></ul>
<!-- /wp:gallery -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>He has been in the academe, real estate and business arena for more than three (3) decades. He was a former Dean of Business Administration, Dean of the Graduate School, and Vice President of Academic affairs of leading universities in Metro Manila and a Professor of Business and Public Administration, Consultant and Business Coach for several Real Estate Companies and Business Corporations. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Furthermore,  conferred diplomate in Business Education, fellow in Personnel Management and fellow in Real Estate Management, Dr. Ong has published 6 books on real estate, business, economics and sociology. He was recently given recognition as Doctor Fellow and International Visiting Professorial Fellow of the Royal Institution of Singapore.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/03/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape-2.png','views' => '101','category_id' => '30','published_at' => '2019-05-27 05:30:55','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '318','title' => 'Schools/Universities Offering BS Real Estate Management in VISAYAS','slug' => 'schools-universities-offering-bs-real-estate-management-in-visayas','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> The Commission on Higher Education (CHED) issued a memorandum (CHED Memorandum Order No. 28) in 2011, outlining the Policies, Standards and Guidelines for Bachelor of Science in Real Estate Management (BS REM). Since its issuance, there have been an increasing number of schools/universities offering the BS REM program. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4135} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/green-chameleon-21532-unsplash-1024x683.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4135"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you are considering to embark on your real estate journey, here are some schools in VISAYAS that offer the program: </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3> <br>University of Cebu</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Sanciangko St., Cebu City  </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/3317/3417238244_9ba45b09cf_b.jpg" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>From a modest start in 1964, the Cebu College of Commerce (CCC) of old has come a long way. Beginning with a handful of enrollees, what used to be an undersized college has successfully evolved into a world-class university in Asia Pacific.<br><br>With 55 years of quality education, the University of Cebu (UC) has been awarded a Deregulated status by the Commission on Higher Education (CHED), while recognized by the Philippine Association of Colleges and Universities Commission on Accreditation (PACUCOA) as a learning institution with the most accredited programs.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Contact Details:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"align":"left"} -->
<p style="text-align:left"> 📍    Sanciangko St., Cebu City </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> 📞   &nbsp;(63-32) 255-7777  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> 📧   <a href="mailto:marian.fajardo@uc.edu.ph">marian.fajardo@uc.edu.ph</a> </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> 🌐    <a href="http://uc.edu.ph/">http://uc.edu.ph</a>  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Central Philippine University </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Lopez Jaena St., Iloilo City </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Central Philippine University is a non-stock, non-profit Christian Institution of higher learning, where a well-rounded program of education is offered under influences that strengthen faith and build up character. It was founded in 1905 as the Jaro Industrial School by missionaries of the American Baptist Foreign Mission Society. It started as an Elementary Vocational School for poor boys who worked for their board and tuition.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In 114 years, CPU has grown from an elementary school with 17 pupils to a widely known university with an enrollment of over 12,000. The 24-hectare campus, which was originally chosen for its quiet and relative isolation, is now a veritable community by itself, with more than 30 buildings used for classrooms and support facilities. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Contact Details:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>📍  Lopez Jaena St.Iloilo City </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>📞  (033) 329 1971 </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>🌐   <a href="https://cpu.edu.ph/">https://cpu.edu.ph</a>  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Hua Siong College of Iloilo </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Iznart St, Iloilo City   </h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<p><img class="alignnone size-large" src="http://huasiong.edu.ph/wp-content/uploads/2015/03/P5260124.jpg" width="617" height="354"></p>

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
