<p><!-- /wp:image --></p>
<p>Following a labor-management dispute in 1994 which was unresolved by the courts, the now-defunct Divine Word University of Tacloban (DWUT), then the region’s biggest university, closed operations.&nbsp;On the same year,&nbsp; Saint Paul’s Business School of Tacloban (SPBST) now Saint Paul School of Business and Law (SPSBL) was founded to accommodate graduating seniors.&nbsp;</p>
<p>Three years later, from a rented and abandoned public wet market set as its campus in Marasbaras, Tacloban City (now the Robinsons Place Tacloban), SPBS transferred to a one hectare space along the Campetic road in Palo, Leyte where a new building stood to serve an expected increase in the enrolment.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;In its 25 years of providing quality tertiary education to Eastern Visayas, SPSBL graduates demonstrated exemplary performances both in Philippine and Global Licensure Examinations.&nbsp; It has produced top-notchers in the Philippine CPA Examination, two Global Top 5 in the Worldwide Certified Internal Auditor Licensure Examination, and a Top Scorer in the Global Certified Information Systems Auditor Licensure Examination.</p>
