<p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></p>','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/05/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape-3.png','views' => '81','category_id' => '11','published_at' => '2019-05-28 00:00:37','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '319','title' => 'How can a foreigner buy a Property in the Philippines?','slug' => 'how-can-a-foreigner-buy-a-property-in-the-philippines','content' => '<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Can a foreigner buy a property in the Philippines? </h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Yes, but with restrictions. Buying a property in the Philippines is possible for a non-Filipino however there are limitations as to what property and how much a non-Filipino can buy. Their ownership is not absolute and subject for restrictions.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>According to the Philippines Anti-Dummy Law, Foreigners (non-Filipinos) can own a property in many ways.  A major restriction in the law is the restriction on the number of foreign members on the Board of Directors of a landholding company (which is limited to 40% foreign participation).&nbsp; </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>What are the properties that they can own in the Philippines? </h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Condominiums<br></h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":3883} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/Evo-City-Entrance-Plaza-night_2016-0510-1024x641.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-3883"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Foreigners are welcome to purchase a condominium in the Philippines. Condominiums work as a corporation. <a href="http://hlurb.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/laws-and-issuances/RA_4726.pdf"> The Condominium Act of the Philippines (R.A. 4726)</a> expressly allows foreigners to acquire condominium units and shares in condominium corporations up to 40% of the total and outstanding capital stock of a Filipino-owned or controlled condominium corporation.  </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Townhouses</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4184} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/courtyards_swimming_pool_with_cascading_waterfall.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4184"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Foreigners can own townhouses as long as it has condominium titles. There are townhouses that offer condominium type of ownership. Like The Courtyards project of Pueblo de Oro Development in Cagayan de Oro City. You can simply inquire from the developers if they are giving condominium titles for the their townhouse projects. If they are, then foreigners can get a townhouse property on that project. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Remember this is only possible for those Townhouse projects with CONDO Titles. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>House and not the Lot</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":1855} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/11988748_1732292993653908_404019646426225835_n.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-1855"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A Foreigner may own a house but not the lot of the Property. And that is why usually for House and lot purchase, they can if they are married to a Filipino spouse.  The title of the land will be named under the name of the Filipino Spouse as the primary owner of the property. <br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Can a foreigner inherit a land and other properties?  That\'s another interesting discussion. I will have to prepare that for you. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For now, share this article to your friends and family. They might find this helpful. Comment below if there are things that needs to be added in the list. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2019/04/filipino-homes-realtor-blogger-xyza-yape-5.png','views' => '56','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2019-05-28 23:00:04','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '320','title' => 'Schools/Universities Offering BS Real Estate Management in MINDANAO','slug' => 'schools-universities-offering-bs-real-estate-management-in-mindanao','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Real Estate Service Act (RESA) requires applicants seeking to be registered and licensed as a real estate service practitioner, except a <a rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="real estate salesperson (opens in a new tab)" href="http://localhost:3001/how-to-be-a-prc-accredited-real-estate-salesperson/" target="_blank">real estate salesperson</a>), to undergo a licensure examination.   One of the requirements to be eligible for the examination is a bachelor\'s degree:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p> A holder of a relevant bachelor\'s degree from a state university or college, or other educational institution duly recognized by the CHED:&nbsp;<em>Provided</em>, That as soon as a course leading to a Bachelor\'s degree in Real Estate Service is implemented by the CHED, the Board shall make this course a requirement for taking the "licensure examination. </p><cite><strong>Section 14.</strong>  <strong><em>Qualification of Applicants for Examinations</em>.</strong>&nbsp;  <br><a href="https://www.lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2009/ra_9646_2009.html">https://www.lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2009/ra_9646_2009.html</a> </cite></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There are a lot of schools/universities offering the BS Real Estate Management (BS REM) program.  If you are considering to start your real estate journey, here are some schools in MINDANAO that offer this course: </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1503676382389-4809596d5290?ixlib=rb-1.2.1&amp;ixid=eyJhcHBfaWQiOjEyMDd9&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;w=1055&amp;q=80" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:separator -->
<hr class="wp-block-separator"/>
<!-- /wp:separator -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Xavier University - Ateneo de Cagayan</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4> Corrales Ave, Cagayan de Oro City</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://www.xu.edu.ph/images/2016/img/may/xu.jpg" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p> Founded in 1933, Xavier University - Ateneo de Cagayan is the first to be granted the University Status (1958) among the five colleges under the Ateneo system in the Philippines, becoming also the first university in Mindanao.<br><br>Today, XU operates on five campuses: the Agriculture extension campus called Manresa, the new campus of the Andrew L Gotianun Sr Center for Integrated Technologies (ALGCIT) along Masterson Avenue, the High School (Junior and Senior) and Grade School Annex in Pueblo de Oro, the Grade School campus in Macasandig, and the main tertiary campus on Corrales Avenue. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
