Join us on April 3, 2020, Friday at 10:00 in the morning for the webinar as Mr. Alejandro S. Mañalac relates to us how he survives different real estate crisis and shares his tips on how to survive the real estate business in this PANDEMIC of COVID-19. ','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/04/91228622_2799974090119313_5257074239848054784_n.jpg','views' => '60','category_id' => '14','published_at' => '2020-03-31 17:09:48','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '407','title' => '10 things you need to understand RESA Law','slug' => '10-things-you-need-to-understand-resa-law','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em>What are the 10 most important things you need to know about the RESA Law or RA 9646?</em></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Are you currently involved in what is considered to be real estate services? If so, your official acts and transactions are covered by Republic Act No. 9646 or the Real Estate Service Act of the Philippines, which is more commonly referred to as the RESA Law. If you are not sure whether your acts constitute a violation of this law, or whether you are even covered by the law, we suggest you read on.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The RESA Law, which took effect on July 30, 2009, deals primarily with the acts generally considered to be real estate services, the qualifications of those who may practice the profession, and the penalties corresponding to violations of its provisions.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The law protects the rights of those who call themselves real estate professionals, and also lays down the rules which these professionals have to adhere to, so that they may be registered under the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC) and be allowed to continue their practice.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>1. Who are those deemed to be practitioners of real estate services and what do they do?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4731,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/76986933_10215716494848117_5656376404464369664_n.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4731"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Section 3(g) of the RESA Law lists five types of professionals who are considered to be in the practice of real estate services. They are real estate consultants, appraisers, assessors, brokers, and salespersons. The first must be duly registered and licensed by the PRC, while real estate salespersons need only be accredited.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>According to the RESA Law, <em><strong>Real Estate consultants</strong></em> are those that offer or render professional advice and judgment on the acquisition, enhancement, preservation, utilization or disposition of lands or improvements thereon. They also facilitate the conception, planning, management, and development of land projects.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><em>Real estate appraisers </em></strong>are those that perform services that involve estimating and rendering an opinion on the worth of the property. <strong><em>Real Estate assessors</em></strong>, on the other hand, are those that are employed by government units (cities, municipalities, and provinces) and appraise and assess the worth of real properties, which include plants, equipment, and machinery. While appraisers perform their tasks for the aim of reporting the values as appraised, assessors perform such tasks for the aim of taxation.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><em><strong>Real estate brokers </strong></em>are those that act as agents of a party in a property transaction. They facilitate the offer, advertisement, solicitation, listing, promotion, mediation, negotiation, or the particular meeting of minds between parties on the sale, exchange, mortgage, lease, joint venture, or any similar transaction involving real properties. in a nutshell, they act as middlemen between the person offering to sell their property and the one offering to purchase it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Finally, <strong><em>Real Estate salesperson</em></strong>s are people who may act on behalf of a real estate broker to facilitate a real estate transaction. it isn\'t uncommon within the Philippines for one land broker to possess several salespersons working under him or her.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>2. What are the qualifications required under RA 9646?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4730,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/77100915_10215663704048380_8759129696140001280_n.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4730"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>All applicants must be Filipino citizens, although the law, in this case, does not distinguish between natural-born and naturalized Filipino citizens. In addition, they must have a college degree (or at least 2 years of college education for salespersons) and have not been convicted of any crime.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In addition to the above-mentioned requirements, consultants must also show proof of experience in the field, including proof of being licensed for at least 10 years as a broker, an assessor, a bank or institutional appraiser, a property-evaluating employee, or as a licensed appraiser for at least 5 years.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Corporations and partnerships are also allowed to engage in real estate practice if they are duly registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission. However, a corporation/partnership’s officers or those authorized to act on its behalf must all be licensed real estate brokers, appraisers, or consultants.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>3. How about professionals who have been practicing real estate services since before 2009</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>According to Section 20, professionals who have been practicing real estate services before the RESA Law took effect in 2009 can be issued a certificate and professional ID without taking the prescribed exam, provided that they satisfy any of the following requirements:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>They are already real estate brokers, appraisers, or consultants licensed by the Department of Trade and Industry</li><li>They are already assessors or appraisers who hold permanent appointments and have been practicing their profession for the last 5 years (before 2009) and have passed the Real Property Assessing Officer exam conducted by the Civil Service Commission</li><li>They are assessors or appraisers who hold permanent appointments, have at least 10 years of actual experience in real property appraisal or assessment, and have completed at least 120 hours of accredited training on real property appraisal</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>4. Does passing the licensure exam automatically lead to the registration?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The PRC may deny your application for the real estate service profession, even if you have passed the licensure exam. The three most common grounds are the following: First, (1) if you have been convicted by a court of law of any criminal offense. Second, if you, upon investigation by the PRC, have been (2) found guilty of immoral or dishonorable conduct. Finally, if you have been (3) found to be psychologically unfit for the profession.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>5. Who is exempted from the application of the law?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Section 28 of the RESA Law stipulates that the following are exempted from the application of its provisions.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>First are <strong>owners of real property</strong> (or private owners), as they are not required to have a license in order to sell their own property. However, this does not include real estate developers, because they are regulated by the Housing and Land Use Regulatory Board (HLURB).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Trustees in bankruptcy or insolvency proceedings </strong>are also exempted, as well as people who act pursuant to court orders (e.g., sheriff of the court); duly constituted attorneys-in-fact authorized to negotiate the sale, mortgage, lease, or exchange of real estate (provided that they do not require any form of compensation); and public officers who perform such acts in line with their official duties. However, it is important to know that the latter does not include government assessors.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>6. Can foreigners practice real estate services in the Philippines?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Generally, no.</strong> However, the law does state an exemption: foreign reciprocity. Section 24 of the RESA Law stipulates that foreigners are prohibited from the practice of real estate services in the Philippines, although it also says that this rule shall not apply if the country where the foreign applicant is from allows Filipinos in the real estate service industry to practice within its territory as its own citizens would.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>7. Are there any other details regarding the licensure examination and the license itself?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The licensure exam is prepared and administered by the Professional Regulatory Board for Real Estate Practice, which is under the PRC. It is composed of 13 real estate-related subjects, which may be increased as the Board sees fit.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>After passing the examination, you will be required to take an oath before a member of the Board or the PRC. In addition, applicants for real estate brokers and appraisers are required to post a cash or surety bond that would serve as professional indemnity insurance. This bond is renewable every 3 years and shall not be less than Php20,000. The license issued is also renewable every 3 years.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The PRC licenses must be displayed in a conspicuous area in the office or business establishment that is dedicated to the practice of real estate services.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>8. Can my license be suspended or revoked?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The PRC may revoke your certificate of registration and professional ID for a number of reasons. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>First, if you got your license through fraud or deceit. </li><li>Second, if you allow an unqualified person to advertise or practice the profession using your own certificate, ID card, or permit. </li><li>Third, if you engage in unprofessional or unethical conduct. </li><li>Fourth, if you commit any form of malpractice or a violation of the RESA Law itself, its Implementing Rules and Regulations, and the Code of Ethics and Responsibilities for Real Estate Service Practitioners. </li><li>And finally, if you get suspended for an offense and continue practicing real estate services.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>9. What does the law prohibit?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong><em>Section 29</em></strong> prohibits the practice or the offer to practice real estate services or to offer yourself as a real estate service practitioner if you have not been licensed by the PRC (except those who are covered by the RESA Law’s exemption).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>10. What are the penalties for violating RA 9646?</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Section 39 stipulates that any violation of the RESA Law, including violations of its Implementing Rules and Regulations, shall be penalized with a fine of not less than Php100,000 or imprisonment of not less than 2 years, or both. If the offender happens to be unlicensed, the aforementioned penalty is doubled (Php200,000 and/or 4 years’ imprisonment).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Furthermore, partnerships, corporations, associations, or other judicial entities who have committed, consented to or knowingly tolerated such a violation are deemed a principal or a co-principal of the violation, along with the other participants, and are subject to the same penalty.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/03/Filipinohomes-blogger-realtor-real-estate-agent-xyza-yape-5.png','views' => '158','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2020-03-31 21:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '408','title' => '4 Effective Ways To Sell Your Property Listing: Tips For Real Estate Agents','slug' => '4-effective-ways-to-sell-your-property-listing-tips-for-real-estate-agents','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Philippines real estate is on steady growth, the property market is not showing signs of another downturn so far, although we are experiencing low inventories lately. And though some countries are experiencing an oversupply of available properties, stagnating prices, and increasing “days on market” the PH market is still competitive in a sense that new projects are coming in, though the ready for occupancy properties are on a scarce right now.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It may not be the same in all parts of the Philippines, there are some areas where the resell rate is high and a number of reselling properties are taking longer to sell these days. Are you a real estate agent who has taken on a lot of listings and has been working for months or years to get them sold? After a long period with no results, the property owners are likely to start calling you. Why hasn’t their property sold? What are you doing to market it? What else are you planning to do to have it sold?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>I would like to share with you a few effective practices and ideas that have helped agents and brokers get their listings sold, and hopefully, these tips can help you, too—whether you need to revive an old listing, or you need to convince a new seller why they need to list with you.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1. Start marketing the moment you sign the listing agreement</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Once you’ve got a contract and secured your position, it’s time to get to work.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Make use of the power of social media</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":2356,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/social-media-1846781_960_720.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-2356"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Share a short preview of your new listing “coming soon to the market” across all your social media channels.<strong><em> </em></strong>Ruby Hamilton, one of the property endorsers of Filipino Homes; applied this strategy in one of her most recent property flips, posting periodic updates on the renovation and its progress on Instagram (an ideal platform to share property photos), being sure to always tag the location, the neighborhood, and a combination of other pertinent keywords, as well as using the hashtag #cdorealestate or #cdoprimeproperties.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Let your fellow agents know</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5213,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Screen-Shot-2020-03-27-at-12.53.30-AM-1024x473.png" alt="" class="wp-image-5213"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Filipino Homes offers an intranet for agents to communicate, post a sneak peek of the property listing with the address and price for fellow realtors to see. They might already have buyers who are considering moving into the neighborhood.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4>Don’t forget to put up the good old real estate sign</h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4575,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/shutterstock_50097079-2ec9cc-630x420.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4575"/><figcaption>Home For Sale Real Estate Sign in Front of New House.</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Place your real estate sales sign as you walk away from the listing meeting with the owner. Add a “coming soon” sign in it, and make sure your contact details is prominently displayed. Anyone driving or passing through the neighborhood will be notified that the property is up for sale soon, and they’ll have a chance to contact you for more information.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2. Stage your listing to make it stand out</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":4570,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Citadines_Cebu_City_Studio_Bed_750x490.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-4570"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In the Philippines, sellers do not really practice staging properties unless it\'s a model unit from a developer. And what makes developer project selling very effective is because of the model units or showrooms that it has prepared and staged for buyers to see and imagine how to design the interior and maximize space. And that\'s how it should also be applied in our brokerage listings. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Unlike in other countries, there are already staging companies that offers lease of furniture and appliances for open house and give options for buyers to include the furnishings in it during the sale. Philippines market is not yet practicing and we agents of Filipino Homes can make a difference in offering the service first in the Philippines. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The sellers are usually not yet open to it because of the possible additional expense that it would take for them to fund the staging, real estate agents can step up and do it for them by partnering with a staging company that would offer consignment of items that they are up for sale in your property listing. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In those days when properties are flying off the shelves, sellers might be reluctant to consider the option of staging their home for sale, as it can be a significant up-front investment. However, when competition is fierce, can you really afford to <em>not </em>stage and let the empty property sit on the market for months?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>From the moment a buyer discovers the home online to the time they walk it during the showing, home staging provides many benefits:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>It creates an impeccable first impression in professional photos.</li><li>It highlights the best features of a property (and can help to hide its shortcomings).</li><li>It allows the visiting buyer to connect with the home on an emotional level and imagine themselves living in it.</li><li>On a completely practical level, the presence of furniture allows the agent and the buyer to sit down and have a conversation, and remain on the property longer.</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Imagine these shelves with nothing on them? It would make for a sterile and unappealing environment for the potential buyer.&nbsp;(C) MIKE BUTLER PHOTOGRAPHY</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>We started staging our flips and it quickly proved to be an indispensable strategy. We have sold homes within as little as 6 days, at or over the asking price, while similar homes sat on the market for 90 days or more, forced to reduce their prices over and over.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Today we stage every one of our flips and I recommend staging to all my real estate clients. We started to offer our home staging services in the Miami and Fort Lauderdale area last year and the results have been very positive. Our latest project scored a cash offer in just two weeks on the market.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>3. Hire a professional photographer who understands architecture and interiors</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/1724198/pexels-photo-1724198.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;h=750&amp;w=1260" alt=""/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Hiring a professional photographer to take photos of your property listing is essential. But beware: there are wannabe professionals and there are real professionals. The first is the kind who might take photos with the window blinds closed or at unflattering angles; the other is someone who knows how to make an interior shot look as good as possible.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Don’t just hire someone with a DSLR camera and who knows Photoshop; hire a photographer who understands interior design, layout, light, and composition.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>What do I mean by that?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A true architectural photographer does not just come in and shoot photos of the space and its elements as they are. He or she moves, adjusts, and places pieces so that the pictures look just right.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="https://thumbor.forbes.com/thumbor/960x0/https%3A%2F%2Fblogs-images.forbes.com%2Fallbusiness%2Ffiles%2F2019%2F03%2Fstaged-property-2-1200x792.jpg" alt="staged livingroom"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>An example of a shoot at one of our staging projects.&nbsp;(C) MIKE BUTLER PHOTOGRAPHY</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The dining table decor as well as the mirror are typically not in the position you see in this picture, but a skilled photographer knows how to rearrange things in order to compose an interesting shot. Photography is both an art and a science—invest in someone who can capture a feeling in their photos.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>4. Give your listing as much visibility as possible</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Of course, don’t forget the basics: publish your listing on the MLS (multiple listing service), syndicate with major real estate platforms like <a rel="noreferrer noopener" href="https://www.zillow.com/" target="_blank"> Filipino Homes</a>, and claim those listings to follow statistics. However, there is more you can do to get more views on your listing.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>List on Facebook Marketplace</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you’re targeting younger buyers, post the home for sale on <a rel="noreferrer noopener" href="https://www.facebook.com/marketplace/" target="_blank">Facebook Marketplace</a>. You’ll be surprised how many inquiries you get from potential buyers!</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Use email lists</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In the United States, where collaboration between real estate agents is very common, do not underestimate the power of your email list. Make it a habit to send the property to your contact list on a regular basis. In the case of our local MLS, I\'m able to pull realtor contact lists and add them to my email base. Make sure you keep the subject line interesting and relevant to increase your email open rate.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Change the featured photo regularly</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This might not be obvious, so I will explain my reasoning. More than 90% of buyers use the internet to search for properties. They will use platforms like Zillow to do so, and they will only see the basic information of your listing and one main photo. Here’s the trick: change that cover photo periodically to attract new attention to your listing. The kitchen may appeal to one person, but the living room to another.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Host open houses</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Open houses have always been and will always be essential when selling a property. They not only allow potential buyers to physically experience the space, but each time you hold one, it makes your listings pop on all online portals and gives your property renewed attention.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Broadcast on Facebook and Instagram Live</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>For those who can’t make it in person, offer a livestream tour of the home. Give your viewers a virtual view of the property and answer their questions in real time. It isn’t just a tour of the property, by the way—they are also getting to know you (and perhaps considering you for their future realtor).</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Reference:  <a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/allbusiness/2019/03/06/real-estate-agent-tips-sell-property-listing-in-buyers-market/?fbclid=IwAR2XU7T3LGWahoC5qBbLQy4V3e1-3z4uxCEkZETxHJDp7iVrZ_eYTGL-Q_M#7c02e2052735 ">Forbes.com</a><br><br></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/03/Filipinohomes-blogger-realtor-real-estate-agent-xyza-yape-6.png','views' => '154','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2020-04-01 21:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:20','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '409','title' => '7 DIY Accent Wall Ideas for Your Home','slug' => '7-diy-accent-wall-ideas-for-your-home','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>How are you all doing a couple of weeks into <a rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="home quarantine (opens in a new tab)" href="http://localhost:3001/metro-manila-under-quarantine/" target="_blank">home quarantine</a>? In case you are running out of things to do, why not <a rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="spruce up your space (opens in a new tab)" href="http://localhost:3001/5-easy-home-improvement-projects-you-can-do-to-keep-you-busy/" target="_blank">spruce up your space</a>  — whether it\'s your living room, bedroom, home office, or bathroom — with an accent wall?  An accent wall is a wall with a design that stands out from the design of the other walls in the room.&nbsp; Here are some DIY ideas you can work on:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1. Bright and Bold Accent Wall</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="https://scontent.cdninstagram.com/v/t51.2885-15/sh0.08/e35/p640x640/31128531_958125641022322_7387289761381089280_n.jpg?_nc_ht=scontent.cdninstagram.com&amp;_nc_ohc=hlz1_YidX5IAX8M5_bE&amp;oh=a9320516704731c028f298fdf5d34beb&amp;oe=5EAB85E8" alt="DIY Accent Wall Ideas | FilipinoHomes"/><figcaption>[<a href="https://www.pinterest.ph/pin/489555421989610738/?nic_v1=1aDIz0qvsd95IOJxFMhsZg0zetrpkavRF7hGhELTtC3hyZraGCIfANhffbDqY3lgNu" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="src (opens in a new tab)">src</a>]</figcaption></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Choosing a bright and bold color is perhaps the easiest way to make a statement in a small space. Paint one wall an energizing color (bright red, orange, or yellow), then use a bold contrast for decor.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2. Bold Black Feature Wall</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","sizeSlug":"large"} -->
