  array('id' => '442','title' => 'How Gen Z Invest, Work and Cope Financially','slug' => 'how-genz-invest-work-and-cope-financially','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Every generation has its own approach in terms of managing finances and investments. Now, those born between 1995 to 2010, collectively referred to as <strong>Generation Z or Gen Z</strong>, are approaching adulthood which will soon enter the labor force.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Interestingly, recent studies reveal that this young generation is actually more informed about investing and more financially conscious than most generations. They were tagged as “frugal”, <em>woked </em>(alert or informed to social injustices), and technology savvy.&nbsp;But with these qualities and the advent of fast-paced digital media, will the Gen Z lead the future of investment?&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sit back, and let’s explore some interesting traits about this generation on how they are shaping the world of business, work, and finance.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>1. Gen Z want to know before diving in</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>These young people are motivated by security. If you are asking why, this may be because they saw how the previous generations took on huge financial hits with recession, economic uncertainties, so on and they learned from it. That’s also why they are taking a much more practical and cautious approach when they do make decisions. They want to be sure to make the right ones or at least be prepared for it.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>2. Gen Z love saving money</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Did you know that <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/3062475/your-guide-to-generation-z-the-frugal-brand-wary-determined-anti-millen">60 percent of the Gen Zers</a> already have a savings account or at least save? This is actually a good indicator for the future of investment made up of mainly teens and young adults. They are likely to be serious about money and career choices. Interesting right?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>3. Gen Z are not “Brand-loyal”</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>It’s not that the brand is dead, but Gen Zers wants to support brands that they believe in and that reflect them. Simply put they are not naive. YOU CAN’T EASILY TRICK THEM. They are completely aware that brands and companies are spending for internet ads or paying influencers to endorse products. By and large, Gen Zers tend to trust individuals more than big institutions. So, if you want them to be loyal to you, be creative, responsive, and build a connection.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>4. Gen Z are more competitive than Millennial</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Gen Z is said to be defined by its competitiveness and being a multi-tasker. They want to work on their own and be judged on their own merits rather than those of their team. This also explains why Gen Zers were tagged as the <strong><em>“Burnout Generation”</em> </strong>to have the worst mental health condition of any generation.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>5. Gen Z prefer independence</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Flexibility is important to them. They prefer to have office space to themselves, rather than an open or a shared workspace. They like to work alone or manage their own projects because they want to get recognized by their skills and abilities, not depending on others too much.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>6. Gen Z want business with a purpose</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>While they are realistic about the challenges ahead, Gen Zers remain optimistic about the future. They want to work for something bigger than them. Something that has an impact on the world. They look for careers that will help the world and focus on businesses or companies that support social issues, as well.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>7. Gen Z advocate for sustainable investing</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>According to Morgan Stanley of Institute for Sustainable Investing, <a href="https://www.morganstanley.com/pub/content/dam/msdotcom/ideas/sustainable-signals/pdf/Sustainable_Signals_Whitepaper.pdf">around 72 percent of Gen Zers</a> expressed desire that responsible investing could improve sustainability outcomes. Gen Zers are becoming more socially responsible, health conscious, and concerned with purchasing environmentally safe products. They are taking action by investing in companies that support their ideals.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>In Summary</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Generation Z is undoubtedly shaping how we do business now.</strong> Composed mainly of teens and early adults, these young people are ambitious, frugal, and tech-savvy. More than that, they value diversity and are entrepreneurial driven by purpose and meaning. And as they enter the professional workforce and start investing, <strong>it will be interesting to see what comes out of their unique approach in the world of money</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you are one of the Gen Z who is considering <a href="http://localhost:3001/category/real-estate-101/">real estate investing</a> as an option, you may talk to a <a href="http://localhost:3001/#">professional real estate agent</a> who knows the ins and outs and the current real estate trends of today.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/How-is-Gen-Z-coping-with-Investments.png','views' => '54','category_id' => '13','published_at' => '2020-06-17 09:29:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '443','title' => 'Why Gen Z should start planning investment now','slug' => 'why-gen-z-should-start-planning-investment-now','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As the <a href="https://www.businessinsider.com/generation-z">Generation Z </a>(or Gen Z) are now approaching adulthood and becoming an asset to the country\'s labor force, it’s the time for this young generation to start thinking about investment. Known for their frugality and “woke minds” for the future, the Gen Z are actually more informed about investing than the Millennials.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Investing can seem complicated but it isn\'t really scary as you think.<em> Personal finance is personal</em>. Your investment entirely depends on your financial goals. But regardless of what that is, the endpoint will always bring considerable benefits. Here are six reasons why you should consider investing now.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>1. More recovery time&nbsp;</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5626,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/1.png" alt="done it before
" class="wp-image-5626"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The beauty of investing now is that you will have more time to make up for any future losses. Risk is absolutely fundamental to investing. This puts you ahead of other investors who start investing at a later stage in life which will get less time to recover his losses.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>2. Develop a good saving and spending habit</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5627,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/2.png" alt="save money" class="wp-image-5627"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Investing is a way of preparing for the future, but you must also not forget to protect your present. To be a good investor is to learn how to save money regularly. While most members of Gen Z are still only teenagers, it’s not too early for this generation to start investing for the future. If you develop a habit of spending less and saving more, you’re already financially ahead from someone who never started. Just remember that consistency is key.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>3. More time for learning</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5628,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/3.png" alt="keep learning" class="wp-image-5628"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Once you are able to establish a good financial habit, and have acquired a protection fund, the next step is to invest in knowledge. In this Digital Age, educating yourself on the best instruments is no longer such a daunting task. Spare a time to learn about the different investments out there such as stock market, bonds, <a href="http://localhost:3001/why-invest-in-real-estate-property/"><strong>real estate</strong></a> and so much more. Having time on your side allows you to choose better investment options that reap great value.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>4. Prepare to be inflation-proof</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5629,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/4.png" alt="inflation i dont care" class="wp-image-5629"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>We know that the prices of goods will always rise through the years, and it’s a pretty normal thing. And the best way to prepare for this eventuality is to invest regularly and <strong>“now” is the time</strong>. If you invest now, you can afford things which others might not in a decade or so. This also explains why investing is better than just saving.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>5. Less dependent on the Government (or anyone)</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5630,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/5.png" alt="i can do it" class="wp-image-5630"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Remember that it’s not the government’s job to cater to our every need, or bail us out of poverty. Most especially during this <a href="http://localhost:3001/how-to-invest-your-money-during-the-coronavirus-pandemic-5-questions-to-ask-yourself-before-investing/">coronavirus pandemic</a> that crashed our economy, we can anticipate more government intervention and debt in which the future generations will pay. That’s why you need to take responsibility on your own and not be dependent on others.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>6. Have peace of mind</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5631,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/6.png" alt="peace of mind" class="wp-image-5631"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There will be times in life when you will need resources to meet unexpected problems like losing a job or an economic recession. And during those unavoidable times, the investment you made now will definitely save you without needing much help from others.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2><strong>Bottom Line:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Now is the time to start investing.</strong> The advantages of planning your investment now will certainly give you more time to recover, develop a good financial habit, have the ability to weather increased risk and opportunities to increase your cash flow, and most especially achieve financial peace of mind. The Gen Z may be in the best position now, but fear the unknown, especially when money is involved. Waiting for the “best time” is a myth, because the longer you wait, the less financial gain you have.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You’re never too young (or old) to invest. Start now when you can. Because sometimes "later" becomes "never".</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/Why-Gen-Z-should-start-planning-for-investments-now.png','views' => '40','category_id' => '13','published_at' => '2020-06-18 09:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '444','title' => '5 Money-Saving Tips for Gen Z','slug' => '5-money-saving-tips-for-gen-z','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Getting started with money-saving or the “saving habit” is the foundation of all financial success. If you are part of the <strong><a href="https://www.businessinsider.com/generation-z">Generation Z</a></strong> (also known as Internet Generation or “Post-Millennial”), it’s important for you to enjoy your NOW, but also not to ignore the importance of saving for the FUTURE. Whether you are in high school, college, or just entering the workforce, <strong>saving money is no excuse</strong>.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Here are six practical money-saving tips that could help you develop a simple and realistic money-saving habit for all your financial goals.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Tip #1 Create a budget system</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5617,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/budget.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5617" /></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There are plenty of budgeting strategies you can take inspiration with. The most common is the 50-30-20 Strategy, wherein you allocate 50% of your income towards your Needs, 30% to Wants, and 20% to Savings &amp; Investments. By following a monthly budget or spending plan (should commit for at least 3 months), it allows you to track your money and ensures that you will always have enough money for the things you need and are important to you. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Tip #2 Budget for an Emergency Fund</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5618,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/EF-1024x683.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5618" /></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Having an emergency fund is a necessity especially for the young ones. An emergency fund is basically a pool of fund or ready cash in case of an unplanned expense, or a loss of a job. The most-recommended budget allocation of an EF is 6 months worth of your income or 3 month’s worth of your usual monthly expenses.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Tip #3 Find ways to reduce your spending</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5619,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/online-shoppinh.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5619" /></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Simple ways like limiting your time on social media is one of the best ways to reduce FOMO or the “Fear of Missing Out” that makes you splurge or overspend. Another tip is to use cash when buying anything less than a thousand pesos. This will help you avoid spending on less important things and encourage you to live within your means.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Tip #4 Make saving money automatic</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5620,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/48054516871_a838efa2c0_b.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5620" /></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This is what they call “paying yourself first.” But how do you do it? Well, there are plenty of ways, like having an automatic savings plan, or a <em>paluwagan </em>system. One simple yet very effective way to automate your money-saving habit is the “50 &amp; 200 Peso bill Challenge”. The rule is simple. If you receive a 50 or 200 pesos bill on hand, that’s considered “<em>unspendable</em>”. Meaning, this goes automatically to your piggy bank. Do this and you’ll be amazed that you’ve reached a 5 digit sum in a span of 6 months. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>Tip #5 Set a savings goal and a reward system</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5621,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/goal.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5621" /></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Having a goal guides you to focus and helps you to sustain that momentum. Start asking yourself what you might want to save for? Perhaps an international trip, a new gadget, <a href="http://localhost:3001/are-you-ready-for-a-home/">a new home</a>, a retirement plan, or a new business. Then try to figure out how much money you’ll need and how long it might take you to save it. More so, creating a reward system will also motivate you in doing your money-saving task. Make sure that whatever rewards you choose makes you motivated and satisfied.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3>Wrapping Up</h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Generation Z are now in the best position to start planning for the future and develop a habit of saving money while young and able. Life is uncertain, thus saving money is a necessity. Creating a budget system, building an emergency fund, and setting up a sound reward system are some of the few things that work well for starters who want to save money. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you\'re interested to know more, here are some <a href="http://localhost:3001/category/tips-guides/">useful tips and guides</a> for you about managing finance, business, and investment. We hope these bits of money saving tips will take you far.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/Best-Money-Saving-Tips-for-iGen-aka.-Gen-Z.png','views' => '48','category_id' => '13','published_at' => '2020-06-19 09:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '445','title' => 'Can I use Maceda Law, if I stopped paying my Loan?','slug' => 'can-i-use-maceda-law-if-i-stopped-paying-my-loan','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>I have been receiving private messages from different people asking about the article which was posted in this blog about Maceda Law. <a href="http://localhost:3001/maceda-law-need-know/">[Click here to read about Maceda Law]</a> </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5054,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/filipinohomes-blogger-realtor-xyzayape4-1024x576.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5054"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A common practice today is for the developers to require only the equity to be paid in installments. This equity or also called “down payment”, varies from 10% to 50% (usually 20%), depending on the developer or the particular development project. The remaining balance after the equity, will be shouldered by some financing scheme.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This financing scheme may be provided by:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li>Banks</li><li><a href="http://localhost:3001/pag-ibig-housing-loan/">HDMF (formerly PAG-IBIG)</a></li><li>“In-house Financing”, by the developer themselves</li><li>or other financing institutions</li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you opt to pay your remaining balance using bank financing, that means you’ll be taking a housing loan from the bank.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>When you start paying to the bank, that means you’ve already taken out your housing loan from them. When you took a loan from your bank, you basically borrowed money and then you used that money to pay the developer in full. But this all happened in the background and the money did not get to your hands anymore. The bank gave it straight to the developer. And this is what commonly confuses people.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>So now, your property has been fully paid as far as the developer/seller is concerned. In fact, as far as the law is concerned, your property has been fully paid already. But your loan from the bank is what’s outstanding. Your debt is now to the bank — the money you borrowed, to pay the developer.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>So the answer to the question on whether this Maceda Law will still apply, is&nbsp;<strong>no, it will not apply anymore</strong>. That’s because the property is technically, already paid in full.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If after reading this post, you still believe you still deserve or want a refund, please speak to a legal counsel for guidance and assistance.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/Filipinohomes-bloggerrealtor-real-estate-agent-xyza-yape-cdo-cebu-iligancity.png','views' => '45','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2020-06-20 07:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '446','title' => 'The Appeal of Smaller Homes to Millennials: 3 Reasons Why the Younger Generation Opt for Tiny, Minimalist Spaces','slug' => 'the-appeal-of-smaller-homes-to-millennials-3-reasons-why-the-younger-generation-opt-for-tiny-minimalist-spaces','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Millennials are the present and the future. They are a driving force in society; they shape and usher in new trends as well as prevail in the working class. In the Philippines alone, millennials comprise a third of the population, with many of them have established careers. Naturally, with a career on the rise, they would be looking for potential investments and one of these is homes of their own. However, contrary to the baby boomers before them, they prefer smaller homes over a sprawling, rich abode. Below are three reasons why smaller homes appeal to millennials. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>Convenience and Proximity over Luxuriousness </strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5679,"width":589,"height":331,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large is-resized"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/conveniencewordcloud-TEASER.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5679" width="589" height="331"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Unlike the older generations who own houses, millennials opt for condominiums for convenience. Equipped with top-of-the-line facilities like gyms, pools, and spaces for recreational activities as well as 24/7 security, condominiums provide the perfect home for the always-on-the-go millennials. The sleek, elegant interiors and perfect views are an added bonus for the generation that favors a clutter-free, minimalist environment that screams aesthetic. More often than not, condos are also nearby to their workplace and places where they can chill and hang out like malls and are easily accessible by public transport.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>Change of Priorities</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5677,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/media-20180228.png" alt="" class="wp-image-5677"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The majority of millennials are in no hurry to settle down and get married. As opposed to the generation that preceded them, their priorities veer more towards personal development, self-care, and becoming financially stable. Establishing families and acquiring properties are way below their bucket list as they go for a much more free-spirited lifestyle. Thus, they prefer to rent than buy as this provides the ease of moving in or out should a job opportunity that would require relocation arise.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":3} -->
<h3><strong>Flexible Payment Methods&nbsp;</strong></h3>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5678,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/calendarcontemporarydigi_140295.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-5678"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As the song goes “one step at a time, there’s no need to rush.” The same goes for millennials and buying property. As opposed to investing all at once, they take a liking to flexible, long-term payment alternatives. Aside from being a more efficient and feasible option, this allows them to seek other opportunities be it pursuing their passions or building a business, without leaving a huge dent in their finances.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Millennials are shaping society as we know it. Perhaps it is time we take a page out of their books and consider choosing smaller homes. After all, despite their petite sizes, their appeal manages to shine through. Perhaps the millennials were onto something after all.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/Filipinohomes-bloggerrealtor-real-estate-agent-xyza-yape-cdo-cebu-iligancity-2.png','views' => '42','category_id' => '12','published_at' => '2020-06-22 11:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '447','title' => 'Find How Much Property You Can Afford','slug' => 'find-how-much-property-you-can-afford','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Finally buying a property of your own? Well, congratulations! You are one step closer to owning your “own” home. But before going through the purchasing phase, take time to sit down and have an honest look at your financial situation. Can you really afford it?</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Most people tend to overlook the most important factor when deciding on buying a property —<em>affordability</em>. They want to buy as much property as they can afford, without being overstretched or seeing the ways forward. Budgeting for a property price isn’t only the expense you have to think of. There are plenty actually. And if you’re wondering if it’s time for you to dive into homeownership, here are a few questions to think about.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container"><!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p>READ: <a href="http://localhost:3001/how-do-you-know-if-you-are-ready-to-buy-a-property-7-financial-questions-to-ask-yourself-before-buying-a-house/">How Do You Know if You are Ready to Buy a Property? [7 Financial Questions to Ask Yourself Before Buying a House]</a></p></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You see it takes the right mind, finance, and attitude to finally own your dream home. Because unlike credit card debt, which can be paid for months or a couple of years, your mortgage payments can continue for decades.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2><strong>How Much House Can I Afford?</strong></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5650,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/how-much.png" alt="" class="wp-image-5650"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Determine the properties you can afford by following rules based on the percentages of your monthly income:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4><strong>The 2.5 Rule</strong></h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This rule is simple. It just means you multiply your annual income by 2.5, to find out which property you can afford. Let’s say, you are making Php50,000 a month (tax and deductions free), your annual income will be Php650,000 (multiplied by 13 as Philippine law requires all companies to pay their employees 13th-month pay); hence, the property you can afford will cost about Php1.625 million.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4><strong>The 28/36 Rule</strong></h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The second rule is the <a href="https://www.investopedia.com/terms/t/twenty-eight-thirty-six-rule.asp">28/36</a>. This states you should only spend a maximum of 28% of your gross monthly income on total housing expenses and should spend not exceeding 36% on total debt service. Anything higher than this can lead to financial stress. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":4} -->
<h4><strong>Other Expenses</strong></h4>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As said above, besides the purchasing price of the property, you still need to set aside a budget for other expenses such as documentary stamp tax, transfer tax, registration fee, notarial fee, loan fee, association dues, and other miscellaneous fees. Don’t underestimate how much money you’ll need. Be ready to have cash above the downpayment amount, and use your calculator to verify your resources and assets.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2><strong>In Summary</strong></h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Buying a property is a significant phase of one’s life. It can be an exciting and emotional experience. But beyond just the price, there are other things you have to consider such as right mind conditioning, preparing finances, as well as the other fees and costs tied to buying a property in the Philippines.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:group -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container"><!-- wp:group -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container"><!-- wp:group -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Disclaimer:</strong> The above advice only provides a general overview of how much an individual can afford, and they should only serve as guidelines. If you need some advice and assistance, <a href="https://filipinohomes.com/properties.php?sparam=forsale">contact any Filipino Homes Property Investment Advisers/Endorsers today</a>.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:group --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>FURTHER READINGS:</strong></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul><li><a href="http://filipinohomes.com/blog/pag-ibig-housing-loan-application-requirements-procedures/">Pag-IBIG Housing Loan Application Requirements and Procedures</a></li><li><a href="http://localhost:3001/real-estate-buying-process-developer/">The Real Estate Buying Process in the Philippines</a></li><li><a href="http://localhost:3001/4-home-financing-options-philippines/">4 Home Financing Options in the Philippines</a></li><li><a href="http://localhost:3001/renting-better-than-buying/">When is Renting Better than Buying a House?</a></li><li><a href="http://localhost:3001/financially-prepared-buy-first-home/">How to be Financially Prepared to Buy Your First Home</a></li></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->','excerpt' => '','featured_image' => 'blog_news/uploads/2020/06/How-Much-Property-Can-I-Afford_.png','views' => '67','category_id' => '13','published_at' => '2020-06-23 11:00:00','created_at' => '2026-01-07 02:04:21','updated_at' => '2026-01-07 02:13:53'),
  array('id' => '448','title' => 'FH holds successful online launching of FilipinoHomes Institute of Real Estate (FIRE)','slug' => 'fh-holds-successful-online-launching-of-filipinohomes-institute-of-real-estate-fire','content' => '<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Last June 12 on the celebration of Philippine Independence Day, <a href="https://filipinohomes.com/">Filipino Homes</a> successfully held its very first ONLINE LAUNCHING of the Filipinohomes Institute of Real Estate (FIRE), an online learning academy for real estate agents and practitioners. This online launching proved also to be a historic debut of the Philippines’ Online Real Estate School!</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5668,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Real-Estate-Institute.png" alt="FilipinoHomes Institute of Real Estate (FIRE)" class="wp-image-5668"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>First announced about two months ago, <a href="https://realestatetraining.ph/">FIRE</a> was borne amidst COVID-19 when the government implemented stay-at-home directives. This led the company to invest in and develop an online real estate academy to adapt to the <em>new normal</em>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"align":"center","id":5579,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="aligncenter size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/FIRE-filipinohomes-institute-for-real-estate.jpg" alt="FIRE - filipinohomes institute for real estate" class="wp-image-5579"/></figure></div>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>During the Facebook Live Grand Launching, <strong>Filipino Homes founder and CEO, Anthony Leuterio</strong>, expressed his gratitude to all the people who witnessed the historic launching of FIRE. He said that <strong>FIRE </strong>was created to aid in developing the skills and talents of real estate practitioners through various courses.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5658,"sizeSlug":"large"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/image-3-1024x576.png" alt="" class="wp-image-5658"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Mr. Leuterio also did a quick virtual tour of the <strong>FIRE <a href="https://realestatetraining.ph/">website</a></strong> guiding the viewers how they can signup and login to the portal, enroll courses, and track progress. He said that the unique feature of FIRE is it\'s simple. It made sure that agents will not have a problem with navigating the online platform. Whether they are a beginner or a refresher, they are welcome to register and enjoy all the available learning resources. Completer will also receive an E-certificate once successfully completed a course.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>About FIRE</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:image {"id":5578} -->
<figure class="wp-block-image"><img src="http://localhost:3001/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/image-1.png" alt="" class="wp-image-5578"/></figure>
<!-- /wp:image -->

<!-- wp:group -->
<div class="wp-block-group"><div class="wp-block-group__inner-container"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Filipinohomes Institute of Real Estate is a program created by Filipinohomes that aids in developing the skills and talents of a real estate practitioner through various online courses. Through this, members of this institute will lead the change in molding the real estate industry through continuous digital learning and becoming better real estate practitioners.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>If you haven’t attended the Live Grand Launching of FIRE via Zoom, check out the video below:&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:core-embed/facebook {"url":"https://www.facebook.com/filipinohomes/videos/268039967794058/","type":"video","providerNameSlug":"facebook","className":""} -->
<figure class="wp-block-embed-facebook wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-facebook"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
https://www.facebook.com/filipinohomes/videos/268039967794058/
</div></figure>
<!-- /wp:core-embed/facebook -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Furthermore, developers and company partners expressed their warmest regards to Filipino Homes for pursuing innovation to help elevate the real estate practitioners to adapt to the “new normal”.&nbsp;</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><p>“Mr. Tonton Leuterio, well what can I say. You’ve always been a trend setter, a frontrunner in industry innovation, and here you are again with FIRE. On behalf of the TAFT Properties, we’d like to send our congratulations to Filipino Homes for again being the first to introduce something that can be really relevant during this time especially to the real estate industry practitioners,”</p><cite>said<strong> Ms. Myra Lynn Gilig</strong>, Vice-President and Chief Operating Officer of TAFT Properties.</cite></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><strong>Filipino Homes is grateful to all its partners, agents, and all the people who in one way or another made this initiative possible. </strong>The company hopes that through this institute, members will lead the change in molding the real estate industry on continuous digital learning and becoming better practitioners. </p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2>REGISTER NOW</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:core-embed/facebook {"url":"https://www.facebook.com/filipinohomes/videos/620280585232567/","type":"video","providerNameSlug":"facebook","align":"center","className":""} -->
